  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how subglacial lakes beneath Antarctica, Iceland, and Greenland may be fueled by different chemical energy sources and redox conditions. In all three settings, ice overlies water and sediment, with microorganisms living in the lake and at the sediment surface. The cartoons emphasize that reduced compounds can diffuse upward from sediments and create chemical gradients that microbes exploit.\n\nIn Antarctica, the lake appears relatively isolated, with oxygen delivered from above and reduced products such as methane and sulfur species coming from the bed. The drawing suggests multiple microbial metabolisms linked to oxygen, nitrate, sulfate, ammonium, methane, and carbon dioxide across the water–sediment boundary.\n\nIceland is shown as strongly influenced by geothermal activity. Upward-moving hydrothermal fluids supply hydrogen sulfide from below, while the lake water is divided into an oxic upper layer and a sulfidic lower layer separated by a chemocline, indicating a pronounced vertical redox gradient.\n\nGreenland is depicted as receiving surface meltwater through a moulin. This input seems to carry oxygen, cells, and labile organic matter into the lake, while methane and other reduced substrates diffuse from sediments below.\n\nA side schematic generalizes these systems as depth-dependent concentration gradients, with reduced compounds increasing where sediment-derived fluxes are strongest.",
